To decouple the Conversation Engine, Context Engine, and Application Orchestrator, you can create separate interfaces or services for each component. Each component can then be developed and tested independently, and their interactions can be defined using interfaces or callbacks.

Here's an example of how you can define the interfaces for each component:

```plaintext
// ConversationEngine interface
interface ConversationEngine {
  void sendMessage(Message message);
  void receiveMessage(Message message);
}

// ContextEngine interface
interface ContextEngine {
  void selectReference(Context context);
  void budgetReference(Context context);
}

// ApplicationOrchestrator interface
interface ApplicationOrchestrator {
  void coordinateConversationEngine(ConversationEngine conversationEngine);
  void coordinateContextEngine(ContextEngine contextEngine);
}
```

Each component can then implement these interfaces and provide their own implementation of the methods. The Application Orchestrator can then call the methods of the ConversationEngine and ContextEngine to coordinate their interactions.

To ensure that the components can communicate with each other, you can use a message passing mechanism. Each component can send messages to the other component using a channel or a queue. The other component can then receive the messages and process them accordingly.

Here's an example of how you can use a message passing mechanism to coordinate the Conversation Engine and Context Engine:

```plaintext
// ConversationEngine implementation
class ConversationEngineImpl implements ConversationEngine {
  private MessageChannel messageChannel;

  public ConversationEngineImpl(MessageChannel messageChannel) {
    this.messageChannel = messageChannel;
  }

  @Override
  public void sendMessage(Message message) {
    messageChannel.sendMessage(message);
  }

  @Override
  public void receiveMessage(Message message) {
    // Process the message
  }
}

// ContextEngine implementation
class ContextEngineImpl implements ContextEngine {
  private MessageChannel messageChannel;

  public ContextEngineImpl(MessageChannel messageChannel) {
    this.messageChannel = messageChannel;
  }

  @Override
  public void selectReference(Context context) {
    messageChannel.sendMessage(new SelectReferenceMessage(context));
  }

  @Override
  public void budgetReference(Context context) {
    messageChannel.sendMessage(new BudgetReferenceMessage(context));
  }
}

// ApplicationOrchestrator implementation
class ApplicationOrchestratorImpl implements ApplicationOrchestrator {
  private MessageChannel messageChannel;

  public ApplicationOrchestratorImpl(MessageChannel messageChannel) {
    this.messageChannel = messageChannel;
  }

  @Override
  public void coordinateConversationEngine(ConversationEngine conversationEngine) {
    conversationEngine.receiveMessage(new SelectReferenceMessage(context));
    conversationEngine.receiveMessage(new BudgetReferenceMessage(context));
  }

  @Override
  public void coordinateContextEngine(ContextEngine contextEngine) {
    contextEngine.receiveMessage(new SelectReferenceMessage(context));
    contextEngine.receiveMessage(new BudgetReferenceMessage(context));
  }
}
```

In this example, the ConversationEngine and ContextEngine classes implement the ConversationEngine and ContextEngine interfaces and provide their own implementation of the methods. The ApplicationOrchestrator class implements the ApplicationOrchestrator interface and provides its own implementation of the methods.

The ApplicationOrchestrator class then calls the methods of the ConversationEngine and ContextEngine to coordinate their interactions. The ConversationEngine and ContextEngine classes receive the messages and process them accordingly.

